<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/04d3ce02f790> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When It Comes to Gorillas, Google Photos Remains Blind" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/04d494ad6998> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google says sorry for racist auto-tag in photo app" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/06c13797e3f9> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos app tags black Jacky Alcine and friend as GORILLAS" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "73" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/09833e715d48> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "For example, April Taylor from iTech Post noted in May that pictures of her dogs had been mislabelled as horses by Google Photos." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0b5cdbcf6036> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Wired tested Google Photos again with a bunch of animal photos." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1136f2e1b0e2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "This is not the first time that facial recognition software, which is based on machine learning and computer vision, has messed up its identification of people." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/121daa17b8c2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google's 'immediate action' over AI labelling of black people as gorillas was simply to block the word, along with chimpanzee and monkey, reports suggest." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/18b4737c527b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google launched its Photos app at Google I/O in May, touting its machine-learning smarts to recognize people, places and events on its own." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1b33deb6da0b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The tagging feature responsible for the mistake is relatively new and has been widely mocked online for other mistakes." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1ceea1bb07b2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos labeled black people 'gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1e3538e00564> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Jacky Alciné checked his Google Photos app earlier this week, he noticed it labeled photos of himself and a friend, both black, as “gorillas.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/20b4cb76d068> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "There is still clearly a lot of work to do with automatic image labeling, and we're looking at how we can prevent these types of mistakes from happening in the future." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/20f02d27278f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Other products that have been criticized include HP's facial-tracking webcams, which could not detect some people with dark skin, and the Apple Watch, which, according to a lawsuit, failed to accurately read blood oxygen levels across skin colors." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/22d9a6c69cd5> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The photo search in Microsoft OneDrive drew a blank for every animal we tried." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/27bd34b044de> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has come under fire recently for an objectively racist “glitch” found in its new Photos application for iOS and Android that is identifying black people as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "79" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/2d0e205e1d27> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "As Zunger notes in a tweet, “Until recently, [Google Photos] was confusing white faces with dogs and seals. Machine learning is hard.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/2f0928193da2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "That’s the conclusion drawn by Wired magazine, which tested more than 40,000 images of animals on the service." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/302d5e0ec9fe> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos Still Has a Problem with Gorillas" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/351b8b4b4129> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "However, it quickly started tracking a white woman's face as soon as she walked in front of the camera." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/39913150ef76> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Yonatan Zunger, Google's chief social architect, responded quickly." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3b9268511837> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Wired tried a number of tests to check the image recognition algorithm ranging from using Google Lens and Google Photos to try and recognise 40,000 images with a variety of subjects and objects." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3cc3c4b63837> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“There is still clearly a lot of work to do with automatic image labeling, and we’re looking at how we can prevent these types of mistakes from happening in the future,” added the Google representative." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "89" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3fcc8c1081e7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "At the same time that product was launched, Flickr released a similar feature, auto-tagging – which had an almost identical set of problems." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4050563ea08f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In 2015, Google drew criticism when its Photos image recognition system mislabeled a black woman as a gorilla—but two years on, the problem still isn't properly fixed." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/40b6e1c07768> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "We discovered Apple Photos had the same issue: It could accurately find photos of particular animals, except for most primates." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/40ca86287be0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "judging by a major blunder by its Photos app this week." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "78" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/44bf8173b750> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“We are taking immediate action to prevent this type of result from appearing. There is still clearly a lot of work to do with automatic image labeling, and we're looking at how we can prevent these types of mistakes from happening in the future.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4779bfd33adc> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[2799] When Google released its stand-alone Photos app in May 2015, people were wowed by what it could do: analyze images to label the people, places and things in them, an astounding consumer offering at the time." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/48b1119b6397> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Margaret Mitchell, a researcher and co-founder of Google’s Ethical AI group, joined the company after the gorilla incident and collaborated with the Photos team." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/517c0dffbee3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When WIRED tested the online demo with gorilla and chimp photos, it identified both." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/528b35e447a7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[73] The internet giant's new Google Photos application uses an auto-tagging feature to help organise images uploaded to the service and make searching easier." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "73" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/58711a788de1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The first fix was not effective so Google ultimately decided not to give any photos a 'gorilla' tag." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5d22c90590f4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google’s Photo App Still Can’t Find Gorillas. And Neither Can Apple’s." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5f576f74d9f4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The same technology allows users to search their personal collections." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5ff8529cf31e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has removed the 'gorilla' tag from its new Photos app, after a user noticed it had filed a number of photos of him and his black friend in an automatically generated album named 'gorillas'." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6097fad2653a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google apologizes for mis-tagging photos of African Americans" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/616682ae2ac9> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Michael Marconi, a Google spokesman, said Google had prevented its photo app from labeling anything as a monkey or ape because it decided the benefit “does not outweigh the risk of harm.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/62ffc0eaeff7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google’s ‘immediate action’ over AI labelling of black people as gorillas was simply to block the word, along with chimpanzee and monkey, reports suggest." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/64968ec3e8bb> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos labels black people as 'gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/650ad2045704> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In a statement, Google spokeswoman Katie Watson said: 'We're appalled and genuinely sorry that this happened.'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/665982941c0e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“We're appalled and genuinely sorry that this happened,\" Google spokeswoman Katie Watson said in a statement to BBC." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6b16280dc668> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Assistant, the ad company’s answer to Apple’s Siri, is also free to call a gorilla a gorilla." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6b522f68d973> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos identified two black people as 'gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6d2492eddaa2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Mistakenly Tags Black People as ‘Gorillas,’ Showing Limits of Algorithms" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "78" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6d8c317abef3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "While we are very proud of this advanced image-recognition technology, we’re the first to admit there will be mistakes and we are constantly working to improve the experience,” a Flickr spokesperson said at the time." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/71feb442f231> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Yet Google, whose Android software underpins most of the world’s smartphones, has made the decision to turn off the ability to visually search for primates for fear of making an offensive mistake and labeling a person as an animal." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/73791a8d766e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "But when we showed it a gorilla, a chimpanzee, a baboon, and an orangutan, Lens seemed to be stumped, refusing to label what was in the image and surfacing only “visual matches” — photos it deemed similar to the original picture." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/75c7afe9fcfb> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "NYPD has been using Clearview AI's facial recognition technology since 2018." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7713f9fe45e1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“Solving these issues is important,” said Vicente Ordóñez, a professor at Rice University who studies computer vision." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/795a67b99db1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Microsoft recently limited users’ ability to interact with a chatbot built into its search engine, Bing, after it instigated inappropriate conversations." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7977a4bf23bf> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google apologizes after app mistakenly labels black people 'gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/79be8f4df568> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google continued to apologize Wednesday for a flaw in Google Photos, which was released to great fanfare in May, that led the new application to mistakenly label photos of black people as “gorillas.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "89" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7c3506324231> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Brooklyn-native Jacky Alcine logged onto Google Photos on Sunday evening, he was shocked to find an album titled \"Gorillas,\" in which the facial recognition software categorized him and his friend as primates." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7e5dce24245e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "There is still clearly a lot of work to do with automatic image labeling, and we're looking at how we can prevent these types of mistakes from happening in the future." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/81c67475b388> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[75] Google has come under fire after the image-recognition feature in its Photos application mistakenly identified people with dark skin as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/8563435074bc> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "A few months after the Nikon controversy, a Youtube video about an HP MediaSmart Computer went viral." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/8b4d72dcade8> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Yonatan Zunger, Google's chief architect of social, was quick to address the problem." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/90d616d5fd96> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In 2015, a black software developer named Jacky Alciné revealed that the image classifier used by Google Photos was labeling black people as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "87" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9308346e7566> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Lens, which tries to interpret photos on a smartphone, also appears unable to see gorillas." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/935ef3f73289> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has said it is \"appalled\" its new Photos app mistakenly labelled a black couple as being \"gorillas\" and has removed the 'gorilla' tag from its new Photos app." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/948d9d1df6e1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google apologises for Photos app's racist blunder" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "81" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/94a100841473> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Apologizes For Tagging Photos Of Black People As ‘Gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/97b519f6ba9a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“There is still clearly a lot of work to do with automatic image labeling, and we’re looking at how we can prevent these types of mistakes from happening in the future.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "79" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/98444aec4024> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Maps made headlines when users discovered last month that entering a racial slur into the search field in some area yielded the address of the White House." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/99135356dc58> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "He added it was \"also working on longer-term fixes around both linguistics - words to be careful about in photos of people - and image recognition itself - eg better recognition of dark-skinned faces\"." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9a1b54aa0c46> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Two years later, Google solves 'racist algorithm' problem by purging 'gorilla' label from image classifier" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "87" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9a1ebe6930a7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Photo apps made by Apple, Google, Amazon and Microsoft rely on artificial intelligence to allow us to search for particular items, and pinpoint specific memories, in our increasingly large photo collections." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9abba03becc8> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The product automatically tags uploaded pictures using its own artificial intelligence software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9adc91e2853c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos identified black people as 'gorillas,' but racist software isn't new" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "79" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/9c7d12acf2e6> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has ‘fixed’ its algorithm that categorised people as 'Gorillas' with a not so elegant solution" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a0e01742729f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google ‘appalled’ as Photos app labels black people ‘gorillas’" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a4345429daa4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Amazon Photos showed results for all searches, but it was over-inclusive." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a8b8baaf5845> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When asked to interpret the same image, it responded: “Hmm… not seeing this clearly yet.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/aeea82726030> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The Nest camera, which used A.I. to determine whether someone on a property was familiar or unfamiliar, mistook some Black people for animals." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b2b53b490b57> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When we showed Lens a photo of a dog, it was able to suggest its likely breed." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bba169efa86d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Years after the Google Photos error, the company encountered a similar problem with its Nest home-security camera during internal testing, according to a person familiar with the incident who worked at Google at the time." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bde8f824df8e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Although it was designed to follow the faces of all users, it couldn't recognize the African-American man moving in front of it." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bf590b14ba48> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "2 years later, Google has simply erased gorillas (and, it seems, chimps and monkeys) from the lexicon of labels its image classifier can apply or be searched with, rendering these animals unsearchable and in some sense invisible to the AI that powers Google's image searching capabilities." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "87" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c4f10653cd41> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos Mistakenly Labels Black People ‘Gorillas’" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "89" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c6dc913263c3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google's solution to accidental algorithmic racism: ban gorillas" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c72e2004e45b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "On Android phones, Google Assistant can be summoned to try and interpret what is on a phone’s screen." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c7520d16d795> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "After Google was criticised in 2015 for an image-recognition algorithm that auto-tagged pictures of black people as “gorillas”, the company promised “immediate action” to prevent any repetition of the error." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/cc5b80e99b5e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Powered by artificial intelligence, the app finds pictures of dogs and tags them \"dog,\" groups snapshots from summer barbecues with one another, and automatically pulls all the pictures of your niece into one folder." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ccff018d8291> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Some of Google’s machine-learning systems are permitted to detect gorillas in public." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/da4fc4756bb6> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "What is interesting is that Google Assistant correctly identified a gorilla as a gorilla." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/dd1705981602> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The fault comes just over a month after Flickr's autotagging system placed potentially offensive tags on images including mislabelling concentration camps as 'jungle gyms' and people as apes." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "73" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e22b9a83980b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has said it is \"genuinely sorry\" after its image recognition software labelled photographs of a black couple as \"gorillas\"." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "88" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e37939c47d4a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Racist tags have also been a problem in Google Maps." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e838d8f482c9> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Lens, a camera app that identifies objects in images, will also refuse to recognise gorillas." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e93ff173b007> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "This past May, Flickr's facial recognition software labeled both black and white people as “animals” and “apes” (these tags were promptly removed)." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ec243931bcb1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The software could identify creatures from pandas to poodles with ease." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/edf7a27eb49d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The Yahoo-owned photo sharing platform labelled a picture of a black man as “ape”, and a photo of the Dachau concentration camp as “jungle gym”." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/effc77ee47a0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos Tags Two African-Americans As Gorillas Through Facial Recognition Software" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/fb5383b1c213> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google was quick to respond over the weekend to a user after he tweeted that the new Google Photos app had mis-categorized a photo of him and his friend in an unfortunate and offensive way." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/fbc672ea2e40> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photo App Labels Black Couple 'Gorillas'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "88" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/Risk/ec4384b7846b> a airo:Risk ;
    rdfs:label "risk of racial misclassification and dehumanizing labeling" ;
    airo:confidenceScore "0.98"^^xsd:float ;
