<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/16101c9f6af2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google's image recognition algorithm created a major controversy after it categorised a black couple as 'Gorillas'." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/17546e66d072> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has removed the 'gorilla' tag from its new Photos app, after a user noticed it had filed a number of photos of him and his black friend in an automatically generated album named 'gorillas'." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1b3d03f40d10> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Last month Flickr also rolled out new technology to help tag photos." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1e2bcfbbd689> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The photo service, launched in May, automatically tags uploaded pictures using its own artificial intelligence software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/2b4ac7ee773e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The internet giant's new Google Photos application uses an auto-tagging feature to help organise images uploaded to the service and make searching easier." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "73" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/333c5b4a0389> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has been forced to apologise after its image recognition software mislabelled photographs of black people as gorillas." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "73" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3e8f86c00813> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google Photos uses sophisticated facial-recognition software to identify not only individuals, but also specific categories of objects and photo types, like food, cats and skylines." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4ab933128417> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has since released a more powerful image analysis product, Google Lens, a tool to search the web with photos rather than text." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4bab10e18b8c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The system is based on machine learning, so as more image data gets fed into the system, the technology will improve and get better at recognising objects in the images." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5e85f7a514ea> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "It uses machine-learning technology to automatically group photos with similar content, say lakes or lattes." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/770c01edb4a6> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "But Google Assistant will correctly identify the primates, as will Google’s business-to-business image recognition service Google Cloud Vision." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b63441e2372f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has come under fire after the image-recognition feature in its Photos application mistakenly identified people with dark skin as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ceee698ab48f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The app uses image labelling technology to assign tags to objects in photos and arrange them into albums." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d2b5bdb34a92> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The failure of the company to develop a more sustainable fix in the following two years highlights the extent to which machine learning technology, which underpins the image recognition feature, is still maturing." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "91" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d3f80742e24c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In fact, the Cloud Vision API, a service that Google's Cloud computing division offers to businesses, was also able to identify chimpanzees and gorillas." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e6198f683d14> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The Google Photos application, launched in May, uses an automatic tagging tool to help organise uploaded images and make searching easier." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "88" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/f319c0a947da> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has said it is \"appalled\" its new Photos app mistakenly labelled a black couple as being \"gorillas\" and has removed the 'gorilla' tag from its new Photos app." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/50992158bf6f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Brian Brackeen, CEO of facial recognition company Kairos, says that machines can make culturally inappropriate assumptions when not properly trained." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/545488414d8e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In 2015, a black software developer named Jacky Alciné revealed that the image classifier used by Google Photos was labeling black people as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "87" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/54d137cec248> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "With enough data and computing power, software can be trained to categorize images or transcribe speech to a high level of accuracy." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/662146afce11> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In May, Flickr’s auto-tagging system came under scrutiny after it labelled images of black people with tags such as “ape” and “animal”." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7301fb093166> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "This issue highlights the complexities and potential problems when it comes to image identification and detection algorithms." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/85422a82b56d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The capability to classify images as containing gorillas remains in some Google products, like Cloud Vision API." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "87" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/85cdbd491616> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Brooklyn-native Jacky Alcine logged onto Google Photos on Sunday evening, he was shocked to find an album titled “Gorillas,” in which the facial recognition software categorized him and his friend as primates." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a5ac8f586927> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Engineers at Google's research labs also recently ran various pictures through its \"neural network\", asking the software to identify patterns in the images and then alter that image to exaggerate the patterns." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "90" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b410d411e64e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The system uses machine learning and as more image data is fed added, the technology should get better at recognising objects in the images." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5298" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bd0b6a5613bf> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Yonatan Zunger, Google’s chief architect of social, responded on Twitter with a promise to fix the tag." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "82" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/daec2c7c33eb> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In another test, WIRED uploaded 20 photos of chimps and gorillas sourced from nonprofits Chimp Haven and the Dian Fossey Institute." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "5301" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/eb0fd05de6e3> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has apologized after its new Photos application identified black people as 'gorillas.'" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ee85aaa8ab3c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Joelle Emerson, founder and CEO of Paradigm, a strategy firm that consults with tech companies on diversity and inclusion, says the incident should be a wake-up call for Silicon Valley." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "92" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/17fbe99aba7f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "We then looked at Google’s competitors. We discovered Apple Photos had the same issue: It could accurately find photos of particular animals, except for most primates." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/683f2f3cfd91> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "About 2 hours later, Yonatan Zunger, whose title at Google is Chief Architect of Social, tweeted Alciné back, asking, \"Can we have your permission to examine the data in your account in order to figure out how this happened?\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7eb7b70dccaa> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The tweet quickly prompted Yonatan Zunger, Google's chief architect of social, to issue an apology." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/919c564b98cb> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Wired tried a number of tests to check the image recognition algorithm ranging from using Google Lens and Google Photos to try and recognise 40,000 images with a variety of subjects and objects." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "84" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a1621f9f7354> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In the case of the Google Photos app — which uses a combination of advanced computer vision and machine learning techniques to help users collect, search and categorize photos — errors are easy to spot." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "89" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a83971a90a47> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Back in 2010, a series of HP computers was widely affected by these so-called \"racist\" webcams." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "79" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c40c71fa4286> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In 2015, Google drew criticism when its Photos image recognition system mislabeled a black woman as a gorilla—but two years on, the problem still isn’t properly fixed." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d175c6f81ca7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "These points are not to shame Google, Nikon, or HP, which are companies that have no malicious intent behind their facial recognition software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d1e617f0e583> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "“We are aware of issues with inaccurate auto-tags on Flickr and are working on a fix." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1184" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/14580c7bdaeb> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Computer vision products are now used for tasks as mundane as sending an alert when there is a package on the doorstep, and as weighty as navigating cars and finding perpetrators in law enforcement investigations." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7348b9396e92> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "When Google released its stand-alone Photos app in May 2015, people were wowed by what it could do: analyze images to label the people, places and things in them, an astounding consumer offering at the time." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/909557090ef4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "\"This is 100% not OK,\" acknowledged Google executive Yonatan Zunger after being contacted by Jacky Alcine via Twitter." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "81" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/bab1c4eebd08> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google has come under fire after the image-recognition feature in its Photos application mistakenly identified people with dark skin as \"gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4204ac9ac713> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Similar software-based gaffes still plague services like Flickr. Last month Flickr rolled out a similar algorithm into its popular photo-sharing network that promised to help users more effectively tag their photos." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "79" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5e8b1dea9704> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "In May, Flickr's auto-tagging feature tagged a black person as an \"ape,\" although it put the same tag on a white woman as well." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6f6be17dd3b8> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "And years ago, some webcams on laptops made by HP didn't track the faces of black people even though they did so for white users." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1183" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1576721630e6> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "A Google spokesperson told CBS News, \"We're appalled and genuinely sorry that this happened. We are taking immediate action to prevent this type of result from appearing.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/432a363b9b08> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The app tagged 2 black people as “Gorillas,” according to Jacky Alciné, a Web developer who spotted the error and tweeted a photo of it." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "78" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5832db0d1dca> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Jacky Alciné of New York City tweeted a picture of himself and a friend on Sunday that the application labelled as \"gorillas,\" a word that also has racist connotations." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "75" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5878a71b509c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "But: Some of Google’s other computer vision systems, such as Cloud Vision, were able to correctly tag photos of gorillas and provide answers to users." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "86" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a28f57a17ca7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Jacky Alciné, a Brooklyn computer programmer of Haitian descent, tweeted a screenshot of Google's new Photos app showing that it had grouped pictures of him and a black female friend under the heading \"Gorillas.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "85" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/d2e09fbd4c1c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google is a leader in artificial intelligence and machine learning." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "78" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/643cb6c34eac> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Back in 2009, Nikon's face-detection cameras were accused of being “racist.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "74" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0c08dd3fbb96> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Google says it is \"appalled\" that its new Photos app mistakenly labelled a black couple as being \"gorillas\"." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "81" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a9347e10521d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Representatives from Amazon and Microsoft said the companies were always seeking to improve their products." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "2799" .

